export const WRITING_EXTRA: WritingPrompt[] = [
  {"id":"gen-w-libraries-sport","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Discuss both views","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people think governments should invest more in public libraries than in sports facilities. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","model":"Public libraries and sports facilities both serve as vital community resources, yet their relative importance is frequently debated. While some argue that governments should prioritize funding for public libraries due to their educational and social benefits, others contend that sports facilities deserve greater investment for their role in public health and community cohesion. Both perspectives have compelling merits, though I believe a balanced approach is most beneficial.  \n\nProponents of increased library funding emphasize their multifaceted role in society. Public libraries provide free access to knowledge, fostering literacy and lifelong learning, particularly for disadvantaged groups who may lack other educational resources. Moreover, they serve as inclusive community hubs, offering quiet study spaces, digital access, and cultural programs that bridge social divides. For instance, libraries often host job-seeking workshops, which directly support economic mobility. Consequently, investing in libraries can yield long-term societal benefits, such as a more informed and skilled workforce.  \n\nConversely, advocates for sports facilities highlight their immediate impact on physical and mental well-being. Regular exercise reduces healthcare burdens by preventing chronic diseases, and communal sports activities strengthen social bonds. In contrast to libraries, which cater primarily to intellectual needs, sports centers address holistic health, particularly in urban areas where recreational space is scarce. A well-maintained public gym or football pitch, for example, can encourage sedentary individuals to adopt healthier lifestyles, alleviating strain on public health systems.  \n\nWhile both institutions are undeniably valuable, I contend that governments should allocate resources equitably rather than favoring one over the other. Libraries and sports facilities address complementary needs—intellectual enrichment and physical vitality—and neglecting either could diminish overall community welfare. Strategic investment in both would ensure a healthier, more educated populace, ultimately yielding broader societal advantages."},
  {"id":"gen-w-video-games","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Positive or negative","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Many young people today spend a lot of time playing video games. Is this a positive or a negative development?","model":"The increasing amount of time young people dedicate to playing video games has sparked debate over whether this trend is beneficial or detrimental. While some argue that gaming fosters valuable skills, I believe the excessive time spent on this activity is largely negative due to its impact on physical health, social development, and academic performance.  \n\nOne significant drawback of prolonged gaming is its adverse effect on physical well-being. Sedentary behavior associated with extended screen time contributes to obesity, poor posture, and eye strain. Moreover, the lack of physical activity exacerbates these health risks, particularly among adolescents who require regular exercise for proper growth. In contrast, engaging in outdoor activities or sports promotes cardiovascular health and overall fitness, which gaming fails to provide.  \n\nAnother concern is the hindrance to social development. While multiplayer games offer virtual interaction, they often replace face-to-face communication, limiting opportunities to develop essential interpersonal skills. For instance, excessive gaming may reduce time spent with family or friends, leading to isolation and difficulty forming meaningful relationships. Consequently, young people who prioritize gaming may struggle with teamwork, empathy, and conflict resolution—skills crucial for personal and professional success.  \n\nFinally, academic performance may suffer as a result of excessive gaming. Time spent on games detracts from studying, completing assignments, or engaging in intellectually stimulating activities. Research indicates a correlation between high gaming hours and lower academic achievement, as prolonged exposure to fast-paced stimuli can shorten attention spans and reduce productivity. However, when played in moderation, certain educational games can enhance problem-solving or strategic thinking, though these benefits are overshadowed by the risks of overindulgence.  \n\nIn conclusion, while video games can offer limited cognitive benefits, the negative consequences—such as health deterioration, impaired social skills, and academic decline—far outweigh these advantages. Parents and educators should encourage balanced screen time and alternative activities to mitigate these adverse effects."},
  {"id":"gen-w-advertising-needs","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people believe that advertising encourages us to buy things we do not really need. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"Advertising is a pervasive force in modern society, influencing consumer behavior through sophisticated psychological techniques. While some argue that it merely informs consumers about available products, I strongly agree that advertising primarily manipulates individuals into purchasing unnecessary items, thereby fostering materialism and financial imprudence.\n\nOne compelling reason for this viewpoint is that advertisements exploit human psychology to create artificial needs. Companies employ strategies such as emotional appeals, celebrity endorsements, and exaggerated claims to convince consumers that their lives are incomplete without certain products. For instance, cosmetic ads often promote unattainable beauty standards, persuading people to buy expensive creams or treatments they would not otherwise consider. Moreover, targeted advertising on social media platforms leverages personal data to present tailored content, making it even harder for individuals to resist impulse purchases. Consequently, many people accumulate goods they rarely use, cluttering their homes and draining their finances.\n\nIn contrast, proponents of advertising claim that it simply provides information, enabling informed choices. While this may hold true for factual advertisements, such as those for public services, the majority of commercial ads prioritize persuasion over education. A stark example is the fast-food industry, which uses vibrant visuals and promotional deals to encourage excessive consumption, despite widespread awareness of its health risks. This demonstrates how advertising often overrides rational decision-making, appealing to desires rather than necessities.\n\nFurthermore, the sheer volume of advertisements in daily life makes avoidance nearly impossible. From billboards to pop-up videos, consumers are bombarded with messages designed to trigger spending. This constant exposure normalizes consumerism, leading many to equate happiness with possession—a mindset that fuels unnecessary purchases.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising undeniably drives people to buy superfluous products by exploiting psychological vulnerabilities and saturating their environment with persuasive content. While it can inform, its primary function is to manipulate, making it a significant contributor to unnecessary consumption. Society would benefit from stricter regulations to curb its more exploitative practices."},
  {"id":"gen-w-living-alone","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Causes & opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"In some countries, more and more people choose to live alone. What are the causes of this, and is it a positive or negative trend?","model":"In recent years, the number of individuals opting to live alone has risen significantly in certain nations. This trend can be attributed to several factors, and while it offers certain advantages, its overall impact is arguably negative due to societal and psychological implications.\n\nOne primary cause of this shift is the increasing financial independence of individuals, particularly women. As gender equality improves and career opportunities expand, more people can afford to sustain a household without relying on shared incomes. Moreover, urbanization has facilitated this trend, as cities provide abundant housing options and amenities that cater to single-person households. Another contributing factor is the growing emphasis on personal freedom and privacy. Many prefer living alone to avoid the compromises inherent in shared living, such as differing lifestyles or schedules. For instance, young professionals often prioritize career growth and personal space over communal living arrangements.\n\nHowever, the societal consequences of this trend are concerning. Living alone can lead to social isolation, which is linked to mental health issues such as depression and anxiety. Humans are inherently social beings, and prolonged solitude may erode community bonds. In contrast, multi-person households foster interpersonal skills and emotional support systems, which are crucial for well-being. Additionally, the environmental impact of single-person homes is significant, as they consume more resources per capita compared to shared living spaces. Consequently, this trend exacerbates urban sprawl and energy inefficiency.\n\nWhile individualism and autonomy are valuable, the decline in communal living may weaken social cohesion. Governments and communities should encourage balanced living arrangements, such as co-housing initiatives, to mitigate these drawbacks. Ultimately, although living alone offers short-term convenience, its long-term effects on society and mental health are detrimental."},
  {"id":"gen-w-practical-skills","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people think schools should teach practical skills such as cooking and managing money. Do you agree or disagree?","model":"Many argue that schools should incorporate practical life skills like cooking and financial management into their curricula. I strongly agree with this viewpoint, as such competencies are essential for independent living and long-term well-being, whereas an overemphasis on theoretical knowledge leaves students unprepared for real-world challenges.  \n\nProponents of traditional education may contend that schools should prioritize academic subjects to cultivate intellectual development. While disciplines like mathematics and literature undoubtedly enhance critical thinking, this perspective overlooks the immediate applicability of practical skills. For instance, a student proficient in calculus may still struggle to budget effectively, leading to financial instability in adulthood. Moreover, basic culinary abilities empower individuals to make healthier dietary choices, reducing reliance on processed foods and associated health risks.  \n\nFurthermore, teaching these skills in schools promotes social equity. Not all children have the opportunity to learn money management or cooking at home, particularly those from disadvantaged backgrounds. By integrating these lessons into the curriculum, schools can bridge this gap, ensuring all students acquire fundamental competencies for self-sufficiency. In contrast, leaving such education to chance perpetuates inequality, as only those with access to familial guidance or extracurricular resources benefit.  \n\nCritics might argue that overcrowded curricula cannot accommodate additional subjects. However, practical skills need not replace academic instruction but rather complement it. For example, mathematics lessons could incorporate budgeting exercises, while science classes might explore nutrition principles applied in cooking. This interdisciplinary approach reinforces theoretical knowledge through real-world application, enhancing overall learning outcomes.  \n\nIn conclusion, schools have a responsibility to equip students with both intellectual and practical tools for adulthood. By teaching essential life skills alongside traditional subjects, they foster well-rounded individuals capable of navigating personal and professional challenges. Consequently, I believe this shift in educational priorities is not only beneficial but necessary."},
  {"id":"gen-w-free-transport","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Governments should make public transport free in big cities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"The proposition that governments should provide free public transport in urban centers is a contentious one. While this policy could yield certain benefits, I fundamentally disagree with the notion, as it would impose excessive financial burdens on authorities while failing to address the root causes of urban transport inefficiencies.  \n\nProponents argue that free public transport would reduce traffic congestion and pollution by incentivizing commuters to abandon private vehicles. For instance, cities like Tallinn, which introduced free buses, reported modest increases in ridership. However, these gains often prove unsustainable without concurrent improvements in service quality. In contrast, simply eliminating fares neglects systemic issues such as overcrowded routes, unreliable schedules, and inadequate infrastructure, which deter usage far more than cost.  \n\nMoreover, the financial implications of such a policy would be staggering. Public transport systems already operate at a loss in most cities, relying on subsidies to function. Removing fare revenue entirely would necessitate either severe cuts to other public services or substantial tax increases, disproportionately affecting lower-income groups who may not even use transit regularly. Consequently, the policy could exacerbate inequality rather than alleviate it.  \n\nA more pragmatic approach would involve targeted subsidies for disadvantaged groups while investing revenue from fares into service expansion and modernization. For example, cities like Singapore and Tokyo maintain affordable yet fee-based systems that are efficient precisely because fares fund continuous upgrades. This model ensures sustainability while addressing the actual barriers to public transport adoption.  \n\nIn conclusion, while the intent behind free public transport is commendable, it is economically unviable and overlooks deeper operational challenges. Governments should prioritize improving existing systems rather than abandoning fare structures altogether."},
  {"id":"gen-w-arts-science","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Discuss both views","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Many people believe that music and art are as important as science at school. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","model":"Music and art have long been integral components of education, yet their importance relative to science remains a subject of debate. While some argue that science is indispensable for technological and economic progress, others contend that music and art are equally vital for fostering creativity and emotional intelligence. Both perspectives have merit, and a balanced view is essential.\n\nProponents of prioritizing science argue that it equips students with critical skills necessary for the modern world. Scientific knowledge drives innovation, improves healthcare, and addresses global challenges such as climate change. Moreover, science education fosters logical thinking and problem-solving abilities, which are highly valued in the job market. For instance, countries with strong STEM (science, technology, engineering, and mathematics) programs often lead in technological advancements and economic growth. Consequently, science is seen as a cornerstone of education, preparing students for careers in high-demand fields.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates for music and art emphasize their role in developing well-rounded individuals. These disciplines nurture creativity, self-expression, and cultural awareness, which are equally important for personal and societal development. Music and art also enhance cognitive abilities, such as memory and spatial reasoning, and provide emotional outlets that improve mental health. In contrast to the rigid structure of science, these subjects encourage students to think outside the box and appreciate diverse perspectives. For example, studies have shown that students involved in the arts often perform better academically, demonstrating the complementary benefits of these disciplines.\n\nIn my view, both science and music/art are indispensable, albeit for different reasons. While science equips students with practical skills, music and art cultivate creativity and emotional intelligence, which are equally crucial in a rapidly changing world. A holistic education system should integrate both, ensuring that students develop not only technical expertise but also the ability to innovate and empathize. Striking this balance is key to fostering well-rounded individuals capable of contributing meaningfully to society."},
  {"id":"gen-w-cinema-home","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Discuss both views","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people prefer to watch films at home, while others prefer the cinema. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","model":"The debate over whether watching films at home or at the cinema offers a superior experience is a matter of personal preference, with valid arguments on both sides. Those who favor watching films in the comfort of their own homes often cite convenience and cost-effectiveness as primary reasons. For instance, streaming platforms provide a vast library of movies accessible at any time, eliminating the need to travel or adhere to fixed screening schedules. Moreover, home viewing allows individuals to pause, rewind, or replay scenes, enhancing their understanding and enjoyment. The ability to personalize the environment, such as adjusting lighting or volume, further contributes to a tailored experience. Consequently, for many, the privacy and flexibility of home viewing make it an appealing choice.\n\nOn the other hand, cinema enthusiasts argue that the immersive experience offered by theaters is unparalleled. The large screens, high-quality sound systems, and darkened auditoriums create a captivating atmosphere that is difficult to replicate at home. In contrast to domestic distractions, such as phone notifications or household chores, cinemas provide an environment where viewers can fully engage with the film. Additionally, the communal aspect of watching a movie with an audience can heighten emotions, as shared laughter or gasps amplify the overall experience. For many, these elements make the cinema a unique and irreplaceable venue for film viewing.\n\nIn my opinion, while both options have their merits, the cinema holds a distinct advantage for those seeking a truly immersive and communal experience. The technical sophistication and emotional resonance of watching a film in a theater outweigh the convenience of home viewing, particularly for highly anticipated releases. Ultimately, however, the choice depends on individual priorities, whether they lean toward comfort and flexibility or the allure of a cinematic spectacle."},
  {"id":"gen-w-work-from-home","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Advantages & disadvantages","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Increasing numbers of people work from home. Do the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","model":"The rise of remote work has become a defining feature of modern employment, driven by advancements in technology and shifting workplace priorities. While this trend offers undeniable benefits, it also presents certain challenges that must be carefully considered. In my view, the advantages of working from home outweigh the disadvantages, provided that individuals and organizations manage the potential drawbacks effectively.\n\nOne significant advantage of remote work is the flexibility it offers. Employees can tailor their schedules to suit their personal needs, enabling a better work-life balance. For instance, parents can manage childcare responsibilities more effectively, and individuals can avoid lengthy commutes, saving both time and money. Moreover, remote work often leads to increased productivity, as employees can design their workspaces to minimize distractions and focus on tasks without interruptions common in traditional office environments. This autonomy fosters a sense of trust and empowerment, which can enhance job satisfaction and morale.\n\nHowever, working from home is not without its challenges. One notable disadvantage is the potential for isolation and reduced collaboration. Without face-to-face interactions, employees may feel disconnected from their colleagues, which can hinder teamwork and creativity. Additionally, the blurring of boundaries between work and personal life can lead to overworking and burnout. For example, individuals may struggle to “switch off” when their home doubles as their office, resulting in prolonged stress and diminished well-being.\n\nDespite these drawbacks, the benefits of remote work can be maximized through proactive measures. Employers can invest in virtual team-building activities and regular check-ins to mitigate feelings of isolation. Furthermore, individuals can establish clear routines and designated workspaces to maintain a healthy balance. Consequently, with proper management, the advantages of remote work—such as flexibility, productivity, and cost savings—can far outweigh its disadvantages.\n\nIn conclusion, while working from home presents certain challenges, its advantages are more significant when effectively addressed. This trend not only aligns with modern technological advancements but also empowers individuals to lead more balanced and fulfilling lives."},
  {"id":"gen-w-tourists-sites","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people think tourists should pay more than local people to enter historical sites. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"The issue of whether tourists should pay higher fees than locals to access historical sites is a contentious one. While some argue that this practice is justified, I firmly believe that imposing such differential pricing is both unfair and counterproductive, as it can create resentment, discourage tourism, and undermine cultural exchange.\n\nProponents of higher tourist fees often argue that these sites require significant maintenance and preservation efforts, which are funded through entrance fees. They contend that tourists, who typically have higher disposable incomes, should bear a larger share of these costs. Moreover, they suggest that locals, who may visit these sites more frequently, deserve discounted rates as a form of cultural entitlement. While these arguments have some merit, they overlook the broader implications of such policies.\n\nOne major drawback of differential pricing is that it can foster a sense of inequality and resentment among visitors. Tourists may perceive higher fees as exploitative, particularly if they are unaware of the rationale behind them. This could tarnish the reputation of a destination and deter future visitors, ultimately harming the local economy. Additionally, such policies may inadvertently discourage cultural exchange. Historical sites are not just tourist attractions; they are also educational resources that promote mutual understanding. Charging tourists more could create a barrier to accessing these valuable cultural assets.\n\nFurthermore, the assumption that tourists have greater financial means is not universally true. Many travelers, particularly budget-conscious ones, may find higher fees prohibitive. In contrast, locals who benefit from discounted rates might take these sites for granted, reducing their appreciation for their own cultural heritage. A more equitable approach would be to implement a uniform fee structure, possibly subsidized by governments or international organizations, to ensure accessibility for all.\n\nIn conclusion, while the need to fund the preservation of historical sites is undeniable, charging tourists more than locals is not the solution. Such practices risk alienating visitors, stifling cultural exchange, and perpetuating inequality. A fair and inclusive pricing strategy would better serve the interests of both tourists and local communities."},
  {"id":"gen-w-neighbours","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Problem & solution","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"People today often know less about their neighbours than in the past. Why is this, and what can be done to improve it?","model":"In contemporary society, it is increasingly common for individuals to have limited interactions with their neighbours, leading to a diminished sense of community. This trend can be attributed to several factors, including technological advancements, urbanization, and changing lifestyles. However, there are practical measures that can be taken to foster stronger neighbourhood connections.\n\nOne significant reason for the decline in neighbourly relationships is the pervasive influence of technology. With the rise of smartphones, social media, and streaming platforms, people often prioritise virtual interactions over face-to-face communication. Consequently, individuals may feel less inclined to engage with those living nearby. Moreover, urbanization has contributed to this issue, as densely populated cities often foster anonymity and a fast-paced lifestyle, leaving little time or inclination for neighbourly bonds. Additionally, the transient nature of modern living, where people frequently relocate for work or education, further weakens long-term community ties.\n\nTo address this issue, proactive steps can be taken at both individual and societal levels. Firstly, community events such as street festivals, potlucks, or sports tournaments can be organized to encourage interaction. These gatherings provide opportunities for residents to meet and build rapport in a relaxed setting. Secondly, local governments or housing associations could initiate neighbourhood watch programs or communal gardening projects, which not only enhance safety and environmental sustainability but also promote collaboration among residents. Furthermore, schools and workplaces could play a role by encouraging students and employees to engage with their local communities through volunteering or outreach programs.\n\nIn conclusion, the erosion of neighbourly relationships is largely driven by technological advancements, urbanization, and transient lifestyles. However, by organizing community events, fostering collaborative projects, and encouraging local engagement, it is possible to rebuild these connections. Strengthening neighbourhood bonds not only enhances social well-being but also contributes to a more cohesive and supportive society."},
  {"id":"gen-w-phones-school","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people believe that children should not be allowed to use smartphones at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"The debate over whether children should be permitted to use smartphones at school has sparked considerable discussion in recent years. While some argue that smartphones can enhance learning and provide valuable resources, I firmly believe that their use should be restricted in educational settings due to the potential for distraction, social issues, and academic underperformance.\n\nOne of the primary reasons for limiting smartphone use is the significant distraction they pose. Children, particularly younger ones, often lack the self-discipline to resist the allure of social media, games, and messaging apps during class. Consequently, their attention is diverted from lessons, leading to reduced comprehension and retention of material. For instance, studies have shown that students who frequently check their phones during lectures tend to perform worse academically compared to those who remain focused.\n\nMoreover, smartphones can exacerbate social issues among students. Excessive use of these devices can lead to cyberbullying, peer pressure, and a decline in face-to-face interactions. In contrast, schools that enforce strict smartphone policies often report improved student relationships and a more inclusive environment. By limiting access to smartphones, educators can foster a sense of community and encourage healthier social dynamics.\n\nAnother compelling argument against smartphone use in schools is its potential to undermine academic integrity. With access to the internet, students may be tempted to cheat on assignments or exams, thereby compromising the fairness of the educational system. Furthermore, reliance on smartphones for quick answers can hinder critical thinking and problem-solving skills, which are essential for long-term academic and professional success.\n\nIn conclusion, while smartphones undoubtedly offer certain educational benefits, their drawbacks far outweigh the advantages in a school setting. By restricting their use, schools can minimize distractions, promote positive social interactions, and uphold academic standards. Therefore, I strongly advocate for policies that limit or prohibit smartphone use during school hours to create a more conducive learning environment."},
];

